1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,492
A brush sweeps away four and a half thousand years of dust.

2
00:00:04,492 --> 00:00:07,605
Underneath lies a brick drain, still in place.

3
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:19,004
In 1921 and 1922, archaeologists began uncovering two forgotten cities, Harappa and Mohenjo-daro.

4
00:00:19,004 --> 00:00:23,741
How did people so long ago build cities this well planned?

5
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:29,929
How do we know?

6
00:00:29,929 --> 00:00:31,730
Almost entirely by digging.

7
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:36,810
The Harappans did write, on seals, but no one has yet deciphered their script.

8
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:40,583
So every fact in this episode comes from archaeology.

9
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:54,588
The Harappan civilisation flourished from about 2600 to 1900 BCE, across a vast region along the Indus and its neighbouring rivers.

10
00:00:54,588 --> 00:00:57,253
Its cities followed a common plan.

11
00:01:01,367 --> 00:01:03,067
Most were divided in two.

12
00:01:03,067 --> 00:01:08,234
To the west stood the citadel: smaller, but built higher on a raised platform.

13
00:01:08,234 --> 00:01:12,786
To the east lay the lower town: larger, where most people lived.

14
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:22,306
Streets were laid out in a rough grid, crossing at right angles.

15
00:01:22,306 --> 00:01:24,314
And the bricks were standardised.

16
00:01:24,314 --> 00:01:32,587
Whether in Harappa or in a town hundreds of kilometres away, bricks kept the same proportions: one unit thick, two wide and four long.

17
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:40,062
The drainage system was remarkable.

18
00:01:40,062 --> 00:01:46,109
Drains ran along the streets, covered with bricks or stone slabs, and house drains connected to them.

19
00:01:52,367 --> 00:01:57,478
Houses were built around courtyards, and many had their own wells and bathrooms.

20
00:01:57,478 --> 00:02:01,565
Doors usually opened onto side lanes, not the main streets.

21
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:12,888
In the citadel of Mohenjo-daro stands the Great Bath: a tank about twelve metres long and seven metres wide,

22
00:02:12,888 --> 00:02:17,131
with steps at each end, made watertight with carefully laid bricks.

23
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:26,550
Many scholars think it was used for ritual bathing.

24
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:32,271
Nearby, and at Harappa, archaeologists found large structures they called granaries.

25
00:02:39,267 --> 00:02:44,862
In India today, you can visit Harappan sites such as Dholavira and Lothal in Gujarat,

26
00:02:44,862 --> 00:02:48,246
Kalibangan in Rajasthan, and Rakhigarhi in Haryana.

27
00:02:48,246 --> 00:02:51,343
Dholavira is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

28
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,217
But were those really granaries?

29
00:02:57,217 --> 00:03:01,868
Some scholars see them as stores for grain, collected as tax or through trade.

30
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:12,904
Others argue the evidence for stored grain is thin, and suggest they may have been halls or warehouses.

31
00:03:12,904 --> 00:03:15,202
The evidence allows both readings.

32
00:03:21,133 --> 00:03:24,166
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

33
00:03:24,166 --> 00:03:26,237
West is high, east is wide.

34
00:03:26,237 --> 00:03:29,126
And every brick is one, two, four.

35
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:36,394
Dig Quiz.

36
00:03:36,394 --> 00:03:38,378
Pause and name the site.

37
00:03:38,378 --> 00:03:42,823
First: a large watertight tank with steps at both ends.

38
00:03:42,823 --> 00:03:45,743
The Great Bath, at Mohenjo-daro.

39
00:03:50,133 --> 00:03:55,238
Second: a brick basin linked by a channel to a river, near the Gulf of Khambhat.

40
00:03:55,238 --> 00:03:57,051
The dockyard at Lothal.

41
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:12,603
Third: a city in three parts, with great stone reservoirs, in the Rann of Kutch.

42
00:04:12,603 --> 00:04:13,296
Dholavira.

43
00:04:13,296 --> 00:04:20,022
In Mains, a classic question asks what Harappan town planning tells us about how the cities were run.

44
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:28,611
Use the grid, the standard bricks and the drains as evidence of planning on a large scale.

45
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:39,727
A standard brick and a drain take their place in the gallery: a city planned four and a half thousand years ago.

46
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:49,218
Next gallery: how the Harappans lived, traded and worshipped.

47
00:04:49,218 --> 00:04:53,242
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

